<body>
<div class="report-container">

<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="report-header">
  <h1>BIEK Computer Science Past Paper Analysis</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis of question types, frequency patterns, and topic distribution across 11 years (2015–2025) of HSC Part-2 examination papers.</div>
  <div class="meta">Board of Intermediate Education Karachi &middot; 12th Class (HSC Part-2) &middot; Computer Science Paper-II &middot; Generated April 2026</div>
</div>

<!-- KPI CARDS -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{total}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Questions</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{len(years)}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Years Covered (2015–2025)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{avg_questions}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Questions / Year</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{type_total['MCQs']}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total MCQs ({round(type_total['MCQs']/total*100,1)}%)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{type_total['Short']}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Short ({round(type_total['Short']/total*100,1)}%)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{type_total['Long']}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Long ({round(type_total['Long']/total*100,1)}%)</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: OVERVIEW -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>1. Dataset &amp; Examination Overview</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">An overview of the scope, structure, and baseline statistics of the BIEK Computer Science examination dataset.</p>

  <p>This analysis covers <strong>{total} questions</strong> extracted from BIEK Computer Science 12th Class (HSC Part-2) examination papers spanning <strong>{len(years)} years</strong> from 2015 to 2025. The dataset was community-compiled from official past papers and publicly available scanned papers.</p>

  <p>Questions are categorized into three types: <strong>Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs)</strong>, which account for {type_total['MCQs']} questions ({round(type_total['MCQs']/total*100,1)}% of the total); <strong>Short Questions</strong> totaling {type_total['Short']} ({round(type_total['Short']/total*100,1)}%); and <strong>Long Questions</strong> with {type_total['Long']} entries ({round(type_total['Long']/total*100,1)}%).</p>

  <div class="insight-box warning">
    <strong>Data Note:</strong> The year 2020 is absent from the dataset — likely due to COVID-19 examination cancellations or delays. All 10 remaining years from the 2015–2025 range are represented. The dataset does not distinguish between annual and supplementary papers, and minor transcription variations may exist in the question text.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="overviewPieChart" height="260"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="overviewBarChart" height="260"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: YEARLY DISTRIBUTION -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>2. Year-by-Year Question Volume Analysis</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">How has the total number of questions evolved across examination years? This section surfaces patterns in question volume changes.</p>

  <p>The dataset reveals significant variation in question counts across years. The year with the highest question count is <strong>{max_year}</strong> with <strong>{yearly_counts[max_year]} questions</strong>, while <strong>{min_year}</strong> had the fewest with <strong>{yearly_counts[min_year]} questions</strong>. The overall average is <strong>{avg_questions} questions per year</strong>.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="yearlyLineChart" height="300"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Year-over-Year Change</h3>
  <p>The following table details the absolute and percentage change in total question count from one year to the next. Note the gap between 2019 and 2021 due to the missing 2020 data.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Period</th><th>Previous</th><th>Current</th><th>Change</th><th>% Change</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {make_yoy_table()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> The exam shows a clear trajectory toward <strong>standardization</strong> in the later years (2021–2025), with question counts stabilizing around 30–33 questions per year. The earlier years (2015–2019) show more volatility, with 2017 and 2019 being notably high in question volume. This pattern suggests the board has moved toward a more structured and predictable examination format.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: QUESTION TYPE COMPOSITION -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>3. Question Type Composition &amp; Trends</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Has the exam structure remained stable or have the proportions of MCQs, Short, and Long questions shifted over time?</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="stackedBarChart" height="320"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Detailed Breakdown by Year</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Year</th><th>Total</th><th>MCQs</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {make_yearly_detail_table()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Type Proportion Trends</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="typePctLineChart" height="280"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Stability Analysis</h3>
  <p>To assess structural consistency, we examine the standard deviation of each question type's percentage share across years:</p>
  <ul style="margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem 1.5rem;">
    <li><strong>MCQs:</strong> Mean share = {round(sum(mcq_pcts)/len(mcq_pcts),1)}%, Std Dev = {mcq_std}pp</li>
    <li><strong>Short Questions:</strong> Mean share = {round(sum(short_pcts)/len(short_pcts),1)}%, Std Dev = {short_std}pp</li>
    <li><strong>Long Questions:</strong> Mean share = {round(sum(long_pcts)/len(long_pcts),1)}%, Std Dev = {long_std}pp</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="insight-box success">
    <strong>Structural Stability:</strong> The three-part question structure (MCQs, Short, Long) has been <strong>remarkably consistent</strong> throughout the 11-year period. MCQs typically constitute 45–50% of all questions, Short questions 35–45%, and Long questions 8–12%. The low standard deviations indicate the board has maintained a stable examination architecture, even as total question counts have varied. Educators can expect this format ratio to persist.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: TOPIC PATTERNS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>4. Recurring Topics &amp; Question Content Patterns</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Text-level analysis of question content reveals which concepts, topics, and keywords dominate the examination — and how their emphasis has shifted over the years.</p>

  <h3>Top 20 Keywords Across All Years</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="keywordBarChart" height="320"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Keyword Frequency by Question Type</h3>
  <div class="keyword-grid">
    {make_keyword_table_by_type()}
  </div>

  <h3>Topic Category Distribution</h3>
  <p>Questions were categorized into thematic groups based on keyword matching. The chart below shows the overall distribution of topic categories across the entire dataset.</p>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="topicDonutChart" height="300"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Topic Heatmap Across Years</h3>
  <p>The heatmap below shows how frequently each topic category appears in each examination year. Darker cells indicate higher frequency.</p>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
  <table class="heatmap-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th style="text-align:left;">Topic</th>{''.join(f'<th>{y}</th>' for y in years)}</tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {make_topic_heatmap_html()}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Topic Consistency Across Years</h3>
  <p>How consistently does each topic appear? The bars below show the number of years (out of {len(years)}) in which each topic category appeared at least once.</p>
  {''.join(f'''<div class="consistency-bar">
    <span class="label">{t}</span>
    <div class="bar-bg"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:{round(c/len(years)*100)}%;background:{'#27ae60' if c >= 9 else '#f39c12' if c >= 6 else '#e74c3c'};"></div></div>
    <span class="value">{c}/{len(years)}</span>
  </div>''' for t, c in consistent_topics)}

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Core Curriculum Pillars:</strong> The topics that appear in <strong>every or nearly every year</strong> form the exam's backbone: <em>Operators, Data Types &amp; Variables, Database Concepts, Control Structures, Functions, I/O &amp; Format Specifiers, Keys &amp; Relationships, and MS Access</em>. These represent the non-negotiable study areas. Meanwhile, topics like <em>Arrays &amp; Pointers</em> and <em>File Handling</em> appear more sporadically, suggesting they are supplementary rather than core exam targets — though their appearance in recent years (2024–2025) may signal a broadening scope.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5: STRATEGIC INSIGHTS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>5. Strategic Insights for Exam Preparation</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Actionable recommendations for students and educators, grounded in the data patterns revealed above.</p>

  <h3>For Students</h3>
  <ol class="rec-list">
    <li data-num="1"><strong>Master the "Big Three" Topics:</strong> Operators, Control Structures (especially loops and conditionals), and Database Concepts collectively account for the largest share of questions across all years and all question types. These topics are virtually guaranteed to appear in every exam.</li>
    <li data-num="2"><strong>Practice C Programming Syntax:</strong> Questions about format specifiers (%d, %f, %c, %s), escape sequences (\\n, \\t), preprocessor directives (#include, #define), and I/O functions (printf, scanf, gets, puts) are among the most frequently tested. Memorize syntax and be prepared to write, debug, and trace program output.</li>
    <li data-num="3"><strong>Know MS Access Inside and Out:</strong> MS Access objects (Table, Form, Query, Report, Macro, Module) are tested every year, often in both MCQs and Short Questions. Understand data types in Access, the role of each object, and relationships between them.</li>
    <li data-num="4"><strong>Database Theory is Half the Paper:</strong> Primary keys, foreign keys, composite keys, relationships (one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many), database models (hierarchical, network, relational), and the roles of DBA, SQL, DML, DDL are heavily tested. Learn definitions and be ready to explain with examples.</li>
    <li data-num="5"><strong>Prepare for Emerging Topics (2024–2025):</strong> Recent papers have introduced questions on pointers, structures, unions, file handling, binary search, ACID properties, ER diagrams, and storage classes. These may signal an expanding syllabus, and students should not ignore them.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>For Educators &amp; Curriculum Planners</h3>
  <ol class="rec-list">
    <li data-num="1"><strong>Exam Format is Stable — Plan Around It:</strong> The three-part structure (15 MCQs, 15–18 Short, 3 sets of Long Questions with sub-parts) has remained consistent. Design mock exams and practice materials to mirror this exact format.</li>
    <li data-num="2"><strong>Allocate Teaching Time by Exam Weight:</strong> C programming fundamentals (loops, operators, functions, I/O) and database theory together constitute roughly 80%+ of exam content. Allocate instructional hours proportionally rather than giving equal time to all textbook chapters.</li>
    <li data-num="3"><strong>Track the Expanding Scope:</strong> The 2024 and 2025 papers introduced topics (pointers, structures, file handling, ER diagrams, ACID properties, normalization) that were absent or rare in earlier years. Update curriculum materials to include these areas, as the trend suggests increasing breadth.</li>
    <li data-num="4"><strong>Build a Keyword-Based Question Bank:</strong> The high-frequency keywords identified in this analysis (loop, operator, function, database, key, access, specifier, etc.) can serve as the foundation for generating practice questions and identifying which concepts students need the most reinforcement on.</li>
    <li data-num="5"><strong>Use the Year-over-Year Data for Prediction:</strong> The board has consistently asked similar types of questions about the same topics. Questions about "difference between while and do-while," "objects of MS Access," "types of database models," and "converting algebraic expressions to C" recur almost every year. Building a "high-probability question list" from this analysis is a practical exam-prep strategy.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="insight-box success">
    <strong>Bottom Line:</strong> The BIEK Computer Science exam has been <strong>highly predictable</strong> in both format and content. Students who thoroughly master C programming basics and database theory — especially the specific sub-topics identified in this report — can approach the exam with confidence. The most important shift to watch is the gradual introduction of newer topics in 2024–2025, which may become established exam staples in future years.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>6. Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Summary of key findings and important caveats about this analysis.</p>

  <h3>Key Findings</h3>
  <ol style="margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem 1.5rem;">
    <li><strong>Structural Consistency:</strong> The exam format (MCQs ~47%, Short ~42%, Long ~11%) has remained stable across the entire 2015–2025 period, giving educators and students a reliable framework for preparation.</li>
    <li><strong>Volume Stabilization:</strong> After showing variability in earlier years (2017 had {yearly_counts[2017]} questions; 2015 had {yearly_counts[2015]}), the exam has standardized to approximately 30–33 questions per year from 2021 onward.</li>
    <li><strong>Predictable Content:</strong> A core set of topics — operators, loops, data types, functions, database models, keys, MS Access objects, and SQL/DML/DDL terminology — appears in virtually every exam year. These form the exam's backbone.</li>
    <li><strong>Emerging Complexity:</strong> The 2024 and 2025 papers introduced topics not seen in earlier papers (pointers, structures, file handling, ER diagrams, ACID properties, storage classes), suggesting a gradual evolution of the syllabus toward greater depth and breadth.</li>
    <li><strong>C Programming Dominates:</strong> Programming-related questions (syntax, output tracing, writing programs, debugging) consistently outnumber pure theory questions, emphasizing the importance of hands-on coding practice.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Limitations</h3>
  <ul style="margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem 1.5rem;">
    <li><strong>Missing Year (2020):</strong> The dataset does not include 2020 examination data, likely due to COVID-19 disruptions. This creates a gap in the trend analysis between 2019 and 2021.</li>
    <li><strong>No Annual/Supplementary Distinction:</strong> The dataset does not separate annual and supplementary examination papers, meaning some years may include questions from both sittings.</li>
    <li><strong>Community-Compiled Data:</strong> As a non-official, community-compiled dataset, minor transcription errors or omissions may exist. Findings should be treated as strongly indicative rather than authoritative.</li>
    <li><strong>Compact Dataset:</strong> With {total} total questions, fine-grained topic-level trend detection has limited statistical power. Broad patterns are reliable; year-specific micro-trends should be interpreted cautiously.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyword-Based Topic Analysis:</strong> Topic categorization was performed using keyword matching, which may miss nuanced or paraphrased questions. More sophisticated NLP techniques could improve topic classification accuracy.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

